      "title": "Compromised bee-js and Swarm Desktop Steal Swarm Wallet Keys",
      "summary": "Attackers published malicious versions of the JavaScript client @ethersphere/bee-js (13.3.0) and Swarm Desktop (0.56.2) through the project's own release pipeline, using a compromised release bot account. The npm package carried a postinstall loader that fetched a second-stage payload, which stole Swarm node wallet keys and installed a backdoor. On Linux and macOS the backdoor was a Go remote shell, and on Windows it was a hidden ScreenConnect client running as SYSTEM. The malicious release still carried valid npm provenance because it was published via a GitHub release, bypassing branch protection. Defenders who installed either version should treat the host as compromised, move wallet funds to a new key, and remove the backdoor.",
