      "report_date": "2026-11-09",
      "title": "A signed fake Windows DLL that asks BITS to keep it alive",
      "summary": "A signed malicious DLL, DUI70.dll, poses as Microsoft's \"Windows DirectUI Engine\" but is signed with a certificate issued to an individual, Barbaros Cetinkaya. It is sideloaded by a genuine Windows program, CameraSettingsUIHost.exe, placed in C:\\Users\\Public\\Documents. The DLL forwards all 86 legitimate DirectUI functions so the host keeps working, and it creates a deliberately failing BITS job whose notify command relaunches the host and reloads the DLL. The DLL then acts as a remote-access backdoor over HTTPS, offering 14 commands including remote shell, file transfer, and process control. Defenders should audit BITS jobs for notify commands pointing outside C:\\Windows, hunt for the listed files in the Public Documents folder, and block the signing certificate.",
